1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,803
The service is slow.

2
00:00:01,803 --> 00:00:04,344
Someone says: it only has a small heap.

3
00:00:04,344 --> 00:00:06,951
Someone else says: it is the wrong port.

4
00:00:06,951 --> 00:00:07,798
Nobody knows.

5
00:00:07,798 --> 00:00:08,905
Everyone guesses.

6
00:00:09,455 --> 00:00:10,882
You do not have to guess.

7
00:00:10,882 --> 00:00:14,534
A running Java process will tell you exactly how it was started,

8
00:00:14,534 --> 00:00:16,645
and what settings it is really using.

9
00:00:17,195 --> 00:00:18,734
Today you learn to ask it.

10
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:20,510
Four questions: is it running,

11
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:21,635
how was it started,

12
00:00:21,635 --> 00:00:23,174
what settings does it use,

13
00:00:23,174 --> 00:00:24,299
and what is hidden.

14
00:00:24,849 --> 00:00:29,115
You will use three small tools: J P S, proc, and J C M D.

15
00:00:31,165 --> 00:00:34,856
Four questions, four tools. All of them read a live process.

16
00:00:35,406 --> 00:00:40,483
One. Is it running? J P S lists Java processes. P grep finds them too.

17
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:41,321
Two.

18
00:00:41,321 --> 00:00:42,691
How was it started?

19
00:00:42,691 --> 00:00:45,863
The proc folder holds the real command line.

20
00:00:45,863 --> 00:00:47,881
J C M D shows the arguments.

21
00:00:48,431 --> 00:00:54,490
Three. What settings does it use? J C M D, V M flags, and V M system properties.

22
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:55,355
Four.

23
00:00:55,355 --> 00:00:56,303
What is hidden?

24
00:00:56,303 --> 00:00:58,260
Defaults chosen by the machine,

25
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:00,471
and the Java tool options variable,

26
00:01:00,471 --> 00:01:02,997
which is added without anyone typing it.

27
00:01:03,547 --> 00:01:05,118
Three flag families.

28
00:01:05,118 --> 00:01:07,397
Dash capital X for the J V M.

29
00:01:07,397 --> 00:01:08,969
Dash X X for tuning.

30
00:01:08,969 --> 00:01:11,483
Dash capital D for your program.

31
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:16,520
Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

32
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:20,027
Start a small server in the background,

33
00:01:20,027 --> 00:01:21,392
with a heap limit,

34
00:01:21,392 --> 00:01:22,832
and two properties.

35
00:01:22,832 --> 00:01:23,666
Type: java,

36
00:01:23,666 --> 00:01:24,500
dash X M X,

37
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:25,638
two five six M,

38
00:01:25,638 --> 00:01:26,472
dash X M S,

39
00:01:26,472 --> 00:01:27,457
sixty-four M,

40
00:01:27,457 --> 00:01:31,248
dash D server dot port equals nine zero nine zero,

41
00:01:31,248 --> 00:01:33,447
dash D currency equals E U R,

42
00:01:33,447 --> 00:01:33,978
server,

43
00:01:33,978 --> 00:01:35,267
and an ampersand.

44
00:01:35,817 --> 00:01:40,169
It prints one line and keeps running. That is a service: it does not exit.

45
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:43,471
Is it running? Type: J P S, dash L.

46
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:47,519
J P S lists Java processes by process I D,

47
00:01:47,519 --> 00:01:48,768
and main class.

48
00:01:48,768 --> 00:01:50,516
It also lists itself,

49
00:01:50,516 --> 00:01:53,514
because J P S is a Java program too.

50
00:01:54,064 --> 00:01:57,072
Another way. Type: P grep, dash A, java.

51
00:01:57,622 --> 00:02:02,870
P grep with dash A prints the process I D and the full command line, flags included.

52
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:06,187
Save the process I D in a variable.

53
00:02:06,187 --> 00:02:07,688
Type: P I D equals,

54
00:02:07,688 --> 00:02:08,241
dollar,

55
00:02:08,241 --> 00:02:09,111
open paren,

56
00:02:09,111 --> 00:02:09,664
P grep,

57
00:02:09,664 --> 00:02:10,217
dash F,

58
00:02:10,217 --> 00:02:10,771
server,

59
00:02:10,771 --> 00:02:11,719
close paren.

60
00:02:12,269 --> 00:02:15,705
Read the real command line from the proc folder.

61
00:02:15,705 --> 00:02:16,421
Type: T R,

62
00:02:16,421 --> 00:02:16,851
quote,

63
00:02:16,851 --> 00:02:17,924
backslash zero,

64
00:02:17,924 --> 00:02:18,354
quote,

65
00:02:18,354 --> 00:02:18,783
space,

66
00:02:18,783 --> 00:02:19,213
quote,

67
00:02:19,213 --> 00:02:19,643
space,

68
00:02:19,643 --> 00:02:20,072
quote,

69
00:02:20,072 --> 00:02:20,788
less than,

70
00:02:20,788 --> 00:02:21,575
slash proc,

71
00:02:21,575 --> 00:02:22,005
slash,

72
00:02:22,005 --> 00:02:22,936
dollar P I D,

73
00:02:22,936 --> 00:02:23,365
slash,

74
00:02:23,365 --> 00:02:24,296
command line,

75
00:02:24,296 --> 00:02:25,513
then a semicolon,

76
00:02:25,513 --> 00:02:26,157
and echo.

77
00:02:26,707 --> 00:02:28,005
The kernel keeps it,

78
00:02:28,005 --> 00:02:29,953
with null bytes between words.

79
00:02:29,953 --> 00:02:31,706
T R turns them into spaces.

80
00:02:31,706 --> 00:02:34,238
This is the truth about how it started.

81
00:02:34,788 --> 00:02:41,358
Ask the J V M itself. Type: J C M D, dollar P I D, V M dot command line.

82
00:02:41,908 --> 00:02:42,952
The J V M flags,

83
00:02:42,952 --> 00:02:43,931
the main class,

84
00:02:43,931 --> 00:02:45,171
and the class path.

85
00:02:45,171 --> 00:02:47,324
Notice the two dash D properties,

86
00:02:47,324 --> 00:02:48,564
and the heap flags.

87
00:02:50,614 --> 00:02:53,131
Go to the shop. Type: CD, shop.

88
00:02:53,681 --> 00:02:55,521
Start the server again,

89
00:02:55,521 --> 00:02:56,161
quietly.

90
00:02:56,161 --> 00:02:57,041
Type: java,

91
00:02:57,041 --> 00:02:57,921
dash X M X,

92
00:02:57,921 --> 00:02:59,121
two five six M,

93
00:02:59,121 --> 00:03:00,001
dash X M S,

94
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:01,041
sixty-four M,

95
00:03:01,041 --> 00:03:05,041
dash D server dot port equals nine zero nine zero,

96
00:03:05,041 --> 00:03:07,361
dash D currency equals E U R,

97
00:03:07,361 --> 00:03:07,921
server,

98
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:10,561
then redirect output to dev null,

99
00:03:10,561 --> 00:03:11,921
and an ampersand.

100
00:03:12,471 --> 00:03:15,197
What settings is it really using?

101
00:03:15,197 --> 00:03:16,354
Type: J C M D,

102
00:03:16,354 --> 00:03:16,932
server,

103
00:03:16,932 --> 00:03:18,089
V M dot flags,

104
00:03:18,089 --> 00:03:18,832
then T R,

105
00:03:18,832 --> 00:03:21,145
then grep for max heap size,

106
00:03:21,145 --> 00:03:22,632
initial heap size,

107
00:03:22,632 --> 00:03:24,119
and use G one G C.

108
00:03:24,669 --> 00:03:27,995
Max heap size is two hundred fifty-six megabytes,

109
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:28,606
in bytes.

110
00:03:28,606 --> 00:03:29,488
Initial heap,

111
00:03:29,488 --> 00:03:30,235
sixty-four.

112
00:03:30,235 --> 00:03:32,610
And the garbage collector is G one.

113
00:03:32,610 --> 00:03:35,189
These are the values in use right now,

114
00:03:35,189 --> 00:03:37,021
not what somebody intended.

115
00:03:37,571 --> 00:03:39,033
And the properties.

116
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:40,110
Type: J C M D,

117
00:03:40,110 --> 00:03:40,648
server,

118
00:03:40,648 --> 00:03:42,648
V M dot system properties,

119
00:03:42,648 --> 00:03:44,955
then grep for server dot port,

120
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:45,955
and currency.

121
00:03:46,505 --> 00:03:48,084
Both properties are there.

122
00:03:48,084 --> 00:03:49,722
Your program can read them.

123
00:03:49,722 --> 00:03:52,393
That is how a dash D flag reaches your code.

124
00:03:52,943 --> 00:03:55,379
What if nobody sets a heap limit?

125
00:03:55,379 --> 00:03:56,191
Type: java,

126
00:03:56,191 --> 00:03:58,553
dash X X plus print flags final,

127
00:03:58,553 --> 00:03:59,513
dash version,

128
00:03:59,513 --> 00:04:01,285
then grep max heap size.

129
00:04:01,835 --> 00:04:05,260
The machine picked a default: a quarter of the memory.

130
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:09,002
A container or a small virtual machine changes this number.

131
00:04:09,002 --> 00:04:11,221
Defaults depend on where Java runs.

132
00:04:11,771 --> 00:04:13,639
One more hidden setting.

133
00:04:13,639 --> 00:04:16,906
Type: Java tool options equals dash X M X,

134
00:04:16,906 --> 00:04:17,996
one hundred M,

135
00:04:17,996 --> 00:04:18,385
java,

136
00:04:18,385 --> 00:04:19,396
dash version,

137
00:04:19,396 --> 00:04:20,174
then head,

138
00:04:20,174 --> 00:04:20,719
dash N,

139
00:04:20,719 --> 00:04:21,030
one.

140
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,489
Picked up Java tool options.

141
00:04:23,489 --> 00:04:25,467
Java adds it to every launch,

142
00:04:25,467 --> 00:04:26,081
silently.

143
00:04:26,081 --> 00:04:28,195
If a service behaves strangely,

144
00:04:28,195 --> 00:04:29,559
check that variable.

145
00:04:31,609 --> 00:04:34,084
Three flag families, and one hidden source.

146
00:04:34,634 --> 00:04:40,586
Dash capital X: memory and J V M behavior. Dash X M X is the biggest heap.

147
00:04:41,136 --> 00:04:45,616
Dash capital D: a property for your program. Written before the class name.

148
00:04:46,166 --> 00:04:51,328
Dash X X: tuning, like the garbage collector. Use defaults until you can measure.

149
00:04:51,878 --> 00:04:54,493
And when the command line looks innocent,

150
00:04:54,493 --> 00:04:56,023
check Java tool options.

151
00:04:56,023 --> 00:04:56,916
Inspect first,

152
00:04:56,916 --> 00:04:57,809
change second.

153
00:04:59,859 --> 00:05:00,776
Your lab task.

154
00:05:00,776 --> 00:05:04,576
Lab seventy-nine starts a small server for you to inspect.

155
00:05:04,576 --> 00:05:06,344
Lab check judges your work.

156
00:05:06,894 --> 00:05:09,085
It lives at git hub dot com,

157
00:05:09,085 --> 00:05:10,649
slash it dash waves,

158
00:05:10,649 --> 00:05:12,448
slash dev ops dash A I,

159
00:05:12,448 --> 00:05:13,309
in academy,

160
00:05:13,309 --> 00:05:13,621
lab,

161
00:05:13,621 --> 00:05:15,577
lab seventy-nine dot S H.

162
00:05:16,127 --> 00:05:18,851
One: start the server with dash X M X,

163
00:05:18,851 --> 00:05:19,926
one nine two M,

164
00:05:19,926 --> 00:05:22,363
server dot port nine one nine one,

165
00:05:22,363 --> 00:05:23,868
and mode equals prod.

166
00:05:23,868 --> 00:05:25,087
Leave it running.

167
00:05:25,637 --> 00:05:30,096
Two: save its real command line from proc into command line dot text.

168
00:05:30,646 --> 00:05:35,851
Three: save the J V M flags, with J C M D, into flags dot text.

169
00:05:36,401 --> 00:05:39,494
Four: save the mode property into property dot text.

170
00:05:40,044 --> 00:05:42,022
Five: answers dot text,

171
00:05:42,022 --> 00:05:43,053
three lines.

172
00:05:43,053 --> 00:05:44,944
Heap flag: dash X M X.

173
00:05:44,944 --> 00:05:46,836
Property flag: dash D.

174
00:05:46,836 --> 00:05:48,727
Inspect tool: J C M D.

175
00:05:49,277 --> 00:05:54,440
Install, then lab up, lab check, and lab down. Lab down stops your server too.

176
00:05:56,490 --> 00:05:58,559
Pass or fail. You need all six.

177
00:05:59,109 --> 00:06:00,923
Five pass lines in lab check.

178
00:06:01,473 --> 00:06:04,459
You can find the process I D of a Java service.

179
00:06:05,009 --> 00:06:06,737
You can read the true command line.

180
00:06:07,287 --> 00:06:09,250
You can name the three flag families.

181
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:11,976
You can show settings from a live process.

182
00:06:12,526 --> 00:06:15,470
lab down, process stopped, nothing left behind.

183
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:17,321
To pass, all six.

184
00:06:19,371 --> 00:06:22,208
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

185
00:06:22,758 --> 00:06:24,849
One: lab up, and read the task file.

186
00:06:25,399 --> 00:06:28,151
Two: start the server with the flags from the task.

187
00:06:28,701 --> 00:06:31,688
Three: save its command line, flags, and property.

188
00:06:32,238 --> 00:06:35,374
Four: also try Java tool options, and compare.

189
00:06:35,924 --> 00:06:38,206
Five: write your answers, and lab check.

190
00:06:38,756 --> 00:06:41,338
Six: lab down, and prove nothing is left.

191
00:06:41,888 --> 00:06:43,722
Pause the video, and begin now.

192
00:06:59,272 --> 00:07:00,147
Quick check.

193
00:07:00,697 --> 00:07:02,745
Which tool lists Java processes?

194
00:07:03,295 --> 00:07:05,727
Where is the real command line of a process?

195
00:07:06,277 --> 00:07:09,477
Which flag sets the maximum heap? Which sets a property?

196
00:07:10,027 --> 00:07:11,669
What is Java tool options?

197
00:07:12,219 --> 00:07:15,910
Objective covered: M C zero nine, objective four.

198
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:18,529
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

199
00:07:29,079 --> 00:07:31,319
Next: jar versus war packaging.

200
00:07:31,869 --> 00:07:35,197
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
